<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerousia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Spartan Senate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gerousia, also called the Spartan Senate, was the council of elders of the ancient Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. In the Archaic and Classical periods it consisted of the two kings and twenty-eight citizens, the gerontes, who had to be at least sixty, were elected by the shouts of the Assembly and held office for life. It was the highest court, judging capital cases, even of kings, and it prepared the measures put to the Assembly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called their election childish, and they were likely drawn from a few aristocratic families. In 227 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> probably made the office annual, and by the reign of Augustus the council had been reduced to twenty-three members.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s political power was divided between three bodies, the Gerousia, the Ephorate and the Assembly. Scholars debate their relative power, but the Gerousia was apparently the most prestigious. Its earliest mention is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-rhetra">Great Rhetra</a>, dated perhaps to about 700 BCE and quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, which fixed its membership at thirty, including the two kings. The Spartans held that the lawgiver Lycurgus had created it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> said it tempered the feverish government of kings with the moderating influence of old age. Scholars assume it descended from a group of advisers to the kings, like the noble councillors of Homeric epic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The twenty-eight gerontes had to be at least sixty, the age at which Spartans stopped army service. They were elected by acclamation: candidates passed before the Assembly, and a jury shut in a windowless building judged whose shouts were loudest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says a newly elected geron crowned himself with a wreath, visited the temples and was feted at banquets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> called the procedure childish and the selection dynastic. The gerontes were probably drawn from a few families that held a de facto monopoly. The kings sat by birth and, entering before sixty, usually served far longer, and a king who was absent could have his closest relative in the Gerousia vote for him.</p><p>The Gerousia was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s highest court and tried capital cases, even those of kings. Pausanias says that in 403 BCE it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> and the other king in the trial of king Pausanias, who was acquitted. It also prepared motions for the Assembly, a power of preliminary deliberation, and guarded the law. In 243 BCE, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, it rejected the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agis-iv">Agis IV</a> by one vote.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 227 BCE Cleomenes III, after abolishing the Ephorate, reduced the Gerousia's power and probably made election to it annual. Pausanias says he destroyed its power and set up a new magistrate, the patronomos. The Gerousia is still mentioned in the exile of twenty-four leading Spartans in 149 BCE. By the reign of Augustus its members numbered twenty-three, and the minimum age had probably dropped to perhaps forty. Inscriptions list the gerontes of each year, but the body had lost much of its authority and prestige.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gerousia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A council of elders, the <b>gerousia</b>, of 28 men over the age of sixty elected for life, sat with the kings, prepared business for the assembly and judged major cases.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek aristocracies were challenged first by tyrants, such as Cypselus, who overthrew the Bacchiadae around 657 BCE, and then by democracy, but rule by the few survived in many cities, and Sparta kept its <b>Gerousia</b>, a council of twenty-eight elders over sixty and the two kings.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Both sat in the council of elders, the <b>Gerousia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
